WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.350
Multi-GPU systems can increase model capacity, throughput, concurrency,

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:09.225
or pipeline flexibility, but they do not automatically become one larger GPU.

00:00:09.225 --> 00:00:11.595
Software must divide models or work explicitly.

00:00:11.595 --> 00:00:14.870
Design around the requirement: fitting one model, accelerating one request,

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:18.555
serving more users, processing batches, or isolating different models.

00:00:18.555 --> 00:00:22.395
Each goal favors different splitting, replication, or routing patterns.

00:00:22.395 --> 00:00:26.490
Account for slots, lanes, topology, spacing, power, cooling, VRAM, context,

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:30.655
device balance, drivers, runtime support, communication, and thermal stability.

00:00:30.655 --> 00:00:32.600
The slowest path can dominate the system.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:37.360
Benchmark single-GPU and multi-GPU configurations with real prompts, context, batch,

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.440
concurrency, and sustained duration.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.580
Measure quality, first token, generation, throughput, memory, utilization, temperature,

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:45.240
power, errors, and recovery.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:50.050
Multi-GPU is worth it when measured workload benefit exceeds added cost and operational

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.045
complexity.

00:00:51.045 --> 00:00:52.980
Sometimes a smaller model, one larger GPU,

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.470
or several simple single-GPU machines is the better engineering choice.
